        <div id="how-to-seal" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How to Apply a Natural Look Paver Sealer
          </h2>
          <p>
            Applying a natural look sealer is straightforward, and because there's no heavy gloss film, mistakes are less visible than with wet-look products. Still, prep is everything. Here's the process for professional results:
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 1: Clean Thoroughly</h3>
          <p>
            Pressure wash the pavers to remove dirt, algae, grease, and grime. Treat any oil or rust stains with a dedicated paver cleaner and pull weeds from the joints. The surface must be spotless — sealer bonds to clean pavers, not to a film of dirt. This matters even more with invisible sealers, because any trapped stains will be locked in permanently.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 2: Re-Sand the Joints</h3>
          <p>
            Top up any joints that have lost sand with fresh polymeric sand, sweeping it in and compacting it. If you're using a matte acrylic or polymer hybrid, it will lock this sand in place. If you're using a pure penetrating sealer, properly installed polymeric sand is what handles stabilization, since the sealer won't.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 3: Let It Dry Completely</h3>
          <p>
            Pavers must be <strong>bone dry</strong> — ideally 24-48 hours after washing with no rain in the forecast. Sealing over trapped moisture is the number one cause of a milky white haze in acrylics. Penetrating sealers also bond best into fully dry, open pores, so don't rush this step.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 4: Test an Inconspicuous Area First</h3>
          <p>
            Because the whole point is to keep the natural look, apply the sealer to a small, hidden test patch and let it cure. Confirm you're happy with the (lack of) color change before committing to the whole patio. This is cheap insurance against surprises on darker pavers.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Step 5: Apply Thin, Even Coats</h3>
          <p>
            Roll or spray the sealer in thin, even coats, working in manageable sections. For penetrating sealers, the goal is saturation without pooling; wipe up any excess that doesn't absorb. For matte acrylics, two thin coats beat one thick coat — flooding it on can cause blushing or an uneven sheen. Keep traffic and water off for the full cure time on the label.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5: Color Change -->
        <div id="color-change" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Will It Change the Color or Look? Managing Expectations
          </h2>
          <p>
            This is the question that brings most people to a natural look sealer in the first place, so let's be honest about it. The answer depends entirely on the type of sealer you choose.
          </p>
          <p>
            A <strong>penetrating silane/siloxane sealer</strong> like the Foundation Armor SX5000 or Eagle Natural Seal leaves <strong>no film and essentially no color change</strong>. Your pavers will look exactly as they do dry — even immediately after rain. This is the right pick if a totally invisible result is non-negotiable.
          </p>
          <p>
            A <strong>matte acrylic</strong> forms a thin film, and a thin film can deepen color very slightly, most noticeably on darker or more porous pavers. It's nothing like the dramatic darkening of a wet-look sealer, but it may not be perfectly invisible either. That's exactly why we recommend a test patch: apply it somewhere hidden, let it cure, and judge the result in dry and wet conditions before doing the whole surface. Set your expectations by the product type, test first, and you'll get a finish you're genuinely happy with.
          </p>
        </div>

