          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Joint Stabilization & Maintenance
          </h2>
          <p>
            For many homeowners, loose joint sand — not appearance — is the real problem: sand washes out with every rain, leaving gaps where weeds and ants move in. Here's where the type of natural look sealer matters most. <strong>Matte acrylics and polymer hybrids</strong> like SuperSeal M, Techniseal Natural Look, and DOMINATOR NL+ bind the sand into a firm matrix to stop washout. <strong>Pure penetrating sealers</strong> don't, so they rely on properly installed polymeric sand to do that job.
          </p>
          <p>
            Whichever you choose, maintenance is simple. Reseal when water stops beading on the surface — typically every <strong>2-3 years</strong>, sooner in full sun or heavy traffic. Penetrating sealers and acrylics both reapply easily over a clean, dry surface; acrylics in particular melt into the previous coat rather than peeling. Keep the joints topped up with sand between coats, sweep regularly, and rinse off spills promptly. Do that, and a natural-look sealed patio will keep its understated good looks for a decade without ever looking wet or artificial.
          </p>
